# TWO ZERO-LENGTH EDGES AT THE SAME PROJECTED POINT, AT DIFFERENT HEIGHTS.
#
#     (0,0,0) (4,0,0) (4,0,0) (4,4,0) (4,4,5) (4,0,5) (4,0,5) (0,0,5)
#              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
#              zero-length at (4,0,0)          zero-length at (4,0,5)
#
# Each of those two edges is a single point of 3-space. They are DISTINCT, so
# they do not intersect -- but they share a projected point, so the broad phase
# keeps the pair, and any two points are trivially collinear, so
# `Prosector::LinesColinearTest` is reached with both segments degenerate. Its
# loop falls through (both are degenerate in all three coordinates) into
# assertions that demand the two coincide. They do not. Abort.
#
# Upstream issue 11. The assertion is wrong, and so is the `return true` below
# it -- two distinct points do not intersect, so the answer is "no".
#
# WHY THIS FIXTURE IS SMALL ON PURPOSE. The crash was found on two lattice knots
# 10^16 apart, where f64 rounding (ULP = 2 at that scale) collapses vertices
# into zero-length edges. But the large coordinates are only a delivery
# mechanism; eight vertices of small-integer doubles reach it directly. The same
# assertion also fired when integral coordinates were transformed by a
# truncated, singular rotation matrix -- one bug behind three symptoms.
#
# NOT reachable from the CLI today: tools/knoodle_io.hpp binds
# LinkEmbedding<Real,Int,float>, the float class, which handles this gracefully
# (the tools report library errors and exit 1). It is reachable now by any
# direct user of LinkEmbedding2/3/4, and it blocks the switchover those classes
# are headed for.
note = two distinct zero-length edges sharing a projected point
knot = unknot (a square with two doubled corners, pushed out of plane)

zero_length_edges      == 2
vertical_edges         == 2
multiple_points        == 1
max_point_multiplicity == 6
spatial                == 0

xcrash_exact     = 2,3,4   | assertion in LinesColinearTest: two distinct degenerate segments (upstream issue 11)
xcrash_cross     = 1,2,3,4 | assertion in LinesColinearTest: two distinct degenerate segments (upstream issue 11)
xcrash_invariant = 2,3,4   | assertion in LinesColinearTest: two distinct degenerate segments (upstream issue 11)
xcrash_symmetry  = 2,3,4   | assertion in LinesColinearTest: two distinct degenerate segments (upstream issue 11)
# The rotation tier splits by COORDINATE TYPE, which is what the `@` scope on a
# marker is for. Under i64 the generic image is a unimodular integer matrix,
# which preserves the degenerate configuration exactly -- so it crashes, same
# issue 11. Under f64/f32 the generic image is a random rotation, which pulls
# the two points to different projected positions before the library ever pairs
# them, and the run instead fails gracefully with error code 3 -- that is
# finding B (a zero-length edge makes its two neighbours share a point of
# 3-space), a different defect with a different outcome.
xcrash_rotation  = 2,3,4 @i64     | assertion in LinesColinearTest: two distinct degenerate segments (upstream issue 11)
xfail_rotation   = 2,3,4 @f64,f32,i32 | zero-length edge makes its neighbours meet in 3D (finding B)
